## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **`data` / `meta` / `links` envelope** | Separates the payload from metadata, leaving room to add fields without breaking clients. |
| **Prefixed string IDs** (`usr_`, `ord_`) | Opaque, non-enumerable, and self-describing in logs. Strings avoid JavaScript integer-precision issues. |
| **Money as integer minor units** (`12497` cents) | Avoids floating-point rounding errors. `currency` makes it unambiguous, and `formatted` is a convenience for display. |
| **ISO 8601 UTC timestamps** | Unambiguous and universally parseable. Clients localize for display. |
| **Order summaries, not full orders** | Keeps the payload light. `previewItems` (e.g., first 2–3) supports a UI card, and `links.self` points to full details. |
| **`itemCount` separate from `previewItems`** | The UI can show "+1 more item" without the full list. |
| **`hasMore` and `totalOrders` in `meta`** | Tells the client whether to show a "View all orders" link. |
| **Empty array, never `null`** | A user with no orders gets `"recentOrders": []`, so clients don't need null checks. |
| **Enumerated `status` strings** | E.g., `pending`, `paid`, `shipped`, `delivered`, `cancelled`, `refunded`. Document them so clients can handle each state. |
| **camelCase keys** | Matches JavaScript clients. Use snake_case instead if your ecosystem prefers it. Just be consistent. |

