# Design: `GET /v1/users/{userId}/profile-with-orders`

## 1. Key design decisions (and why)

| Decision | Choice | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Resource shape | Composite object: `user` + `orders` | One round trip for a profile page. See §6 for when to split. |
| Order list wrapper | `orders: { items: [], page: {} }` | Lets you add pagination metadata later without changing the array's type (a bare array is a breaking change the moment you need a cursor). |
| Money | Integer minor units + ISO 4217 currency | Never floats. `12400` = £124.00. Currency is explicit per order, not inherited. |
| Timestamps | RFC 3339 / ISO 8601, UTC, `Z` suffix | Unambiguous, sortable, parseable everywhere. |
| IDs | Opaque prefixed strings (`usr_`, `ord_`) | Prevents enumeration, leaks no row counts, allows ID-type changes. |
| Nullability | Omit fields with no value; use explicit `null` only for semantically meaningful empty states (`cancelledAt: null`) | Distinguishes "not applicable" from "not yet happened". |
| Sensitive data | Excluded by default | No `passwordHash`, `internalNotes`, `fraudScore`, full PAN. `last4` only. |
| PII | `dateOfBirth` behind a separate scope/endpoint | Date of birth is a strong identity signal; don't ship it in a default profile payload. |

---

